      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2679.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 670,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to truly understand water, it helps to look at it through a few different lenses: its unique physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Unique Physical Properties\nWater is one of the most unusual and remarkable substances in the universe. \n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). This is why it can transport vital nutrients through our bodies and through the soil.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the **\"Blue Planet\"** because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n\n* **Where it is:** The vast majority of Earth's water (about 96.5%) is saltwater found in the oceans. Only about 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrologic (water) cycle. It evaporates from the oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to the earth as rain or snow, and flows back into bodies of water. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Essence of Life\nWater is the fundamental building block of life as we know it. \n\n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, and our lungs are about 83%. We need it to regulate body temperature, flush out waste, lubricate joints, and carry oxygen through our blood. \n* **Ecosystems:** Every known living thing—from tiny bacteria and giant redwood trees to blue whales and humans—requires water to survive. Without water, biochemical reactions within cells cannot take place.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It shapes our planet's geography, drives our weather and climate, and makes biological life possible."
